              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 76-year-old male patient, admitted on 08/06/2009 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically suspicious for appendicitis. Diagnostic workup including laboratory studies revealed elevated inflammatory markers (CRP, leukocytosis), and abdominal ultrasound confirmed appendiceal inflammation. CT abdomen was performed to exclude complications given his age. The diagnosis of acute appendicitis was confirmed. The patient underwent uncomplicated laparoscopic appendectomy on 09/06/2009 under general anaesthesia. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis without perforation. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. He received perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis and adequate analgesia. Wound healing was satisfactory. The patient was mobilised early and discharged home on 12/06/2009 in good general condition. Please arrange follow-up in two weeks. Should you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact me.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
